<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nathaniel Isaacs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as uDambuza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nathaniel Isaacs was an English adventurer, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> into a Jewish family, who played an important part in the history of Natal. Stranded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Port Natal</a> in 1825 when his ship foundered, he lived among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, met King Shaka regularly and fought in the ranks of his warriors, and was rewarded with land near present-day Durban and made an induna, a headman, under Shaka. His Travels and Adventures in Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> (1836), whose accuracy is often disputed, became a principal source for the history of Natal. He left in 1831, later built a business from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> and ruled Matakong Island, and in 1854 was accused of slave-trading but escaped prosecution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nathaniel-isaacs.jpg" alt="Nathaniel Isaacs" width="190" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nathaniel-isaacs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of what is known comes from European traders such as <b>Nathaniel Isaacs</b> and Henry Francis Fynn, whose accounts historians now read with caution, and from the Zulu oral histories James Stuart collected around 1900.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
